  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   We aim to improve the current bound by further optimizing the step function. The central idea is to combine local optimization with gradient information from the worst shift to direct the search. This approach leverages the structure of the problem while maintaining feasibility constraints. The current construction is refined by:\n     - Smooth interpolation of values\n     - Local optimization focusing on worst shifts\n     - Progressive tightening of constraints\n   Limitations include the approximated gradient and convergence risks, but these are mitigated by multiple restarts.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - n_points = 280 (if parent available)\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211(h[i] * dx) = 1\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - Formulation: use step function h(x) on [0, 2] with integral 1\n   - Optimization: local search via gradient projection at worst shifts\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Validate parent solution (if provided)\n   - Generate random feasible starting points\n   - For each candidate:\n        Time \u2a7d (2/3)*budget_s\n        Compute worst shift k_max and gradient g_max\n        Use projected gradient descent with backtracking\n        Update candidate if improvement\n   - Final polishing step\n   - Output best solution (h_values) and overlap bound\n\n4. Search Choices\n   - n_points = 280 (fixed for consistency)\n   - Random points: uniform over [0,2] with constraint projection\n   - Local maxiter: 1000 (within time budget)\n   - ftol: 1e-6 for convergence\n   - Gradient step size: adaptive backtracking\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Multi-start local search within time budget\n   - Time check inside optimization\n   - Fall back to parent if local search fails\n   - Gradient computed only for worst shift\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Check h[i] \u2208 [0, 1]\n   - Verify integral within 1e-4\n   - Final overlap computation re-runs\n   - Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - c5_bound recalculated with updated correlation",
